<node id="idea:A1-01">
  <data key="d0">idea</data>
  <data key="d1">Grid-hum liveness: real-time Electrical Network Frequency (ENF) matching to authenticate voice-confirmed high-value instructions</data>
  <data key="d2">A1</data>
  <data key="d3">2026-09-30</data>
  <data key="d4">fraud</data>
  <data key="d5">Voice clones now pass voice biometrics and human call-backs (a bank wired $108M after a cloned lawyer's voice confirmed the instruction). Every current check analyses the voice itself, which is exactly what generative models imitate.</data>
  <data key="d6">When a high-value instruction is confirmed on a call, the bank media server isolates the mains-hum component that microphones pick up incidentally (fundamental 50/60 Hz and surviving harmonics 100-180 Hz in wideband/VoIP codecs), tracks its instantaneous frequency with a high-resolution estimator, and derives rate-of-change-of-frequency (RoCoF) features over several simultaneous windows (0.1 s, 1 s, 10 s). It compares these against live phasor-measurement-unit (PMU) grid-frequency data for the caller's claimed grid region over the same timestamps. Grid frequency random-walks unpredictably, so a live call from a powered room in the claimed region correlates, while synthetic TTS/voice-conversion audio carries no hum, a looped or stale hum, or a region-mismatched trajectory. Using multiple windows prevents the short-window grid events (steps) from being averaged away, which is the insight from inertia measurement. The ENF-consistency score is fused as an independent physical factor in the instruction's authorisation.</data>
  <data key="d7">0.262</data>
</node>
