<node id="idea:A1-02">
  <data key="d0">idea</data>
  <data key="d1">Camera-free RF occupancy sensing at ATMs to detect coercion and 'helper' card-swap fraud without biometric identification</data>
  <data key="d2">A1</data>
  <data key="d3">2026-09-30</data>
  <data key="d4">fraud</data>
  <data key="d5">Distraction and 'helper' frauds at ATMs (a second person watching the PIN, then swapping the card) and coerced withdrawals are common, especially against the elderly. Camera-based fixes create biometric-misidentification risk: people have been wrongly accused of bank fraud from face-recognition matches.</data>
  <data key="d6">The ATM uses its existing Wi-Fi/BLE radio, or a low-cost 60 GHz radar module, to measure channel-state-information perturbations in a defined zone around the fascia during a session. A lightweight model estimates how many bodies are within 0.8 m, where they are relative to the PIN pad, and how they move (leaning in during PIN entry, lingering after card return, a hand approaching the card slot). The transaction state machine consumes this occupancy stream. If multiple occupancy coincides with PIN entry and a high withdrawal, or a second body approaches during card return, the ATM switches to privacy-PIN mode (a randomised keypad layout shown only at a narrow viewing angle), delays dispensing behind an on-screen prompt, requires in-app confirmation, or raises a silent alert. Only anonymous occupancy traces are retained as evidence, so a person can never be falsely identified from them.</data>
  <data key="d7">0.2333</data>
</node>
