<node id="idea:A1-03">
  <data key="d0">idea</data>
  <data key="d1">Verification-aware payment scheduler using virtual output queues and iterative matching to remove urgency-driven verification bypass</data>
  <data key="d2">A1</data>
  <data key="d3">2026-09-30</data>
  <data key="d4">payments</data>
  <data key="d5">Social-engineering scams (such as AI-generated executive messages) exploit cut-off pressure. Instructions awaiting out-of-band verification block FIFO payment queues, and operators under time pressure release them unverified.</data>
  <data key="d6">The payment hub keeps a virtual queue per (verification resource, risk class) pair instead of one FIFO. Each scheduling slot, an iterative request-grant-accept matcher with rotating round-robin pointers (as in crossbar switch schedulers) allocates scarce verification resources to pending instructions: call-back agents, biometric re-authentication, dual-control approvers. This guarantees bounded wait, no starvation and no head-of-line blocking of clean payments. An instruction whose verification cannot complete before its cut-off is rescheduled deterministically to the next settlement cycle and the customer is told why. The scheduler has no code path that releases an unverified flagged instruction, so urgency cannot accelerate a flagged payment.</data>
  <data key="d7">0.261</data>
</node>
