<node id="idea:A1-04">
  <data key="d0">idea</data>
  <data key="d1">Cryptographic co-presence receipts from concurrent UWB ranging for card-present, corporate-approval and exculpatory evidence</data>
  <data key="d2">A1</data>
  <data key="d3">2026-09-30</data>
  <data key="d4">identity</data>
  <data key="d5">Remote impersonation (CEO fraud, relay attacks on cards) and false accusations after biometric misidentification both come down to one question: was this person's device physically there? Today there is no verifiable record of presence.</data>
  <data key="d6">Terminals (ATMs, branch tablets, POS, and office anchors used for treasury approvals) run a concurrent coded ranging protocol with registered phones: binary transmit/listen codes let many phones in a busy lobby range at the same time instead of one after another, using IEEE 802.15.4z scrambled timestamp sequences for secure distance bounding. For each transaction, both secure elements sign a co-presence receipt {terminal id, device key id, distance upper bound, timestamp, transaction hash}. Receipts are used to (a) require the registered device within X m for card-present or cardless cash, which defeats relay and stolen-card use; (b) require approvers' devices near an office anchor for high-value corporate releases; and (c) serve as cryptographic alibi or corroboration in disputes and investigations. Only receipt hashes go into an append-only log, and the owner reveals a receipt with consent.</data>
  <data key="d7">0.2454</data>
</node>
