<node id="idea:A1-05">
  <data key="d0">idea</data>
  <data key="d1">Latent-space equalisation bridge: exchanging scam-risk embeddings between independently trained models of banks, telcos and platforms without retraining or raw data</data>
  <data key="d2">A1</data>
  <data key="d3">2026-09-30</data>
  <data key="d4">fraud</data>
  <data key="d5">Romance and investment scams are visible early on messaging platforms and telco networks, but the bank only sees the final transfer (e.g. 'Mum sent them £140,000'). Sharing raw conversations or call data is legally impossible, and participants will not co-train one shared model.</data>
  <data key="d6">Each participant keeps its own, independently trained risk encoder. Using a small set of consented anchor entities that appear across participants (known scam numbers, payees, domains, plus benign controls), the bridge fits a lightweight equaliser for each directed pair (A→B): an orthogonal Procrustes or low-rank map with a nonlinear residual that sends A's latent vectors into B's latent space. At run time A sends only a latent vector for an entity (a phone number, payee account, URL), optionally with differential-privacy noise. B equalises it into its own space and feeds its existing classifier unchanged. The bridge monitors how well the anchors reconstruct; when drift passes a threshold after either side updates its model, it re-fits the equaliser automatically. In practice, a messaging platform's grooming-risk representation of the payee's contact reaches the bank's payment-risk model at the moment of transfer.</data>
  <data key="d7">0.2426</data>
</node>
