<node id="idea:A1-06">
  <data key="d0">idea</data>
  <data key="d1">Living QR: flicker-coded payment codes read by event-based vision sensors to defeat QR swaps, stickers and screenshot replay</data>
  <data key="d2">A1</data>
  <data key="d3">2026-09-30</data>
  <data key="d4">payments</data>
  <data key="d5">QR payments dominate India and much of Asia, and QR tampering is a volume fraud: stickers pasted over merchant codes, screenshot replay, fake 'receive money' QRs. A static optical code carries no liveness, and dynamic codes that change every 30 s can still be photographed and relayed.</data>
  <data key="d6">The payee display renders a standard-looking QR, so ordinary cameras still work, but modulates a subset of modules with an imperceptible high-frequency ternary luminance flicker (+/0/−, 0.5–2 kHz). The flicker carries a rolling nonce stream and a MAC bound to the payment request and merchant key. The payer device reads it with an event-based vision sensor (or a high-speed camera mode), which captures polarity events asynchronously and is not limited by frame rate. Verification requires the nonce to be current to within milliseconds and the MAC to match the QR payload. A printed sticker, photograph or screenshot has no flicker channel, and a relayed live stream adds measurable latency, so both fail. The same channel carries larger payloads such as a signed invoice or merchant certificate without dense codes.</data>
  <data key="d7">0.2515</data>
</node>
