<node id="idea:A1-07">
  <data key="d0">idea</data>
  <data key="d1">Certified residual control of AI shopping agents: hardware-enforced bound on how far an agent's spending can drift from the user's mandate</data>
  <data key="d2">A1</data>
  <data key="d3">2026-09-30</data>
  <data key="d4">payments</data>
  <data key="d5">On-device agentic commerce (chipmaker plus card-network partnerships) lets LLM agents pay. Mandates and limits are checked one transaction at a time, but prompt injection or hallucination can push many individually valid purchases that together drift far from what the user meant.</data>
  <data key="d6">The signed user mandate is compiled into a deterministic nominal policy, a planned spending trajectory per category over time. The LLM agent may only propose residual actions relative to that plan. A safety filter inside the device TEE / secure element projects each proposed residual onto the set that keeps a certified ℓ2 (energy) bound on the cumulative deviation between actual and nominal trajectories. The bound comes from an offline stability certificate for the policy, as in residual learning on top of a certified controller. Only the projected action can request an agentic network token. The token carries an attestation of the certificate and the remaining deviation budget, so the issuer can verify compliance without seeing the prompt. Whatever the model outputs, total harm is capped by the certified budget.</data>
  <data key="d7">0.2146</data>
</node>
