<node id="idea:A1-08">
  <data key="d0">idea</data>
  <data key="d1">Body-area motion consensus: authorising wallet or tokenized-deposit payments only when co-worn earbuds, watch and phone agree they share one moving body</data>
  <data key="d2">A1</data>
  <data key="d3">2026-09-30</data>
  <data key="d4">identity</data>
  <data key="d5">A phone can be stolen while unlocked, handed over under coercion, or operated by a 'helper'. Device-bound payment credentials then work for the wrong person. Biometric prompts add friction and are also coerced.</data>
  <data key="d6">At authorisation, the phone collects 2–4 s IMU windows from enrolled co-worn devices over authenticated BLE: earbud head IMU, watch, and smart insoles if present. A reliability-gated fusion model scores each stream's reliability, dropping biased, drifting or disconnected ones, and tests a body-kinematic consensus hypothesis: gait phase, head-trunk coupling and step timing must be consistent with one body. If consensus holds with enrolled wearables, step-up is skipped. If the phone moves but the wearables are stationary or out of sync (phone snatched, handed over, or operated by another person), the payment is limited, delayed, or sent for confirmation on the wearable.</data>
  <data key="d7">0.2254</data>
</node>
