<node id="idea:A2b-01">
  <data key="d0">idea</data>
  <data key="d1">Unpredictable-quorum confirmation: bank-drawn random co-approvers contacted on bank-initiated channels for out-of-topology treasury instructions</data>
  <data key="d2">A2b</data>
  <data key="d3">2026-09-30</data>
  <data key="d4">fraud</data>
  <data key="d5">A cloned executive voice plus a fake WhatsApp thread moved €95M overseas. Attackers script the impersonation of one or two known approvers in advance.</data>
  <data key="d6">For each corporate client the bank learns the instruction topology: who instructs, through which channel, in what approval order, and for which beneficiary classes and geographies. Any instruction whose path through this graph is new (for example a first overseas beneficiary requested by voice or messaging, or an approver skipping their usual channel) is held for a quorum. At confirmation time the bank draws k co-approvers at random from the client's authorised signatory list using a verifiable random function. It contacts them only through bank-initiated channels on enrolled devices (in-app push with passkey signature), never through the channel the instruction came in on. The attacker cannot know in advance whom else to impersonate, and has to compromise several devices in real time. The VRF output is logged so the selection can be audited.</data>
  <data key="d7">0.2657</data>
</node>
